#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084516 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 5 &= x \\cdot 3 \\\\\n10 &= 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 3x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3} &= \\frac{3x}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "89ea4830-364e-5105-8df0-54cf9f7e1e36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084517 :tutorial/content "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\\\[5pt] 3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\\\[5pt] 24 &= -18x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 24 &= -18x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d61fcf8b-f639-5c4c-8997-66813230bedf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8780 :tutorial/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Negative Signs"}]}
